IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `sacat-dates`

-- South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) -- baseline encoding from the seat of the applicant:
-- the person who brings a claim or a matter before the South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (SACAT), or
-- asks it to review a decision made about them, and goes on to an internal review, an appeal or enforcement. Most
-- answers hold equally for another party (a respondent); where they differ the question says so.
--
-- SEAT. The applicant. Questions are phrased as that person asks them. The Tribunal, its President and members, the
-- registrar, the decision-maker and the Supreme Court get no seat: their opinions, satisfactions, leave and orders are
-- asked as facts ("the Tribunal has given leave", "the Tribunal is satisfied that ...").
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The duties with a deadline (s 35(2) with regulations r 6, the decision-maker's reasons and documents within 21
-- days; ss 70(2) and 71(3), my internal review application and my appeal within 1 month) are regulative rules run inside
-- a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: ss 3(1) (applicant, decision, monetary order), 4, 29, 32-38, 38B(2), (5)-(7), 38C(1), (2),
-- 38I(1), (2), 40(4), 41(3), 46-49, 50(10), (13), 51(11), (12), 56(1), (2), (4), 57, 58(1), 60(1), (2)(e), (3), 62(4),
-- 66, 70, 71(1)-(3), 73, 84, 85, 89(1)-(3), 91, 93A, 93B; South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal
-- Regulations 2015 rr 4, 5, 6, 7, 9, 11, 14(1)-(7). What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Act version 1.6.2025 and Regulations version 1.7.2020, both fetched from www.legislation.sa.gov.au on
-- 6 November 2026 (unauthorised RTF), deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/ and
-- ../../../south-australian-civil-and-administrative-tribunal-regulations-2015/registers/source-bundle/.
--
-- Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website at 6 November 2026. State of South Australia,
-- CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Tribunal, the President, a
-- registrar or the Supreme Court are INPUTS. Answers are named outcomes: a "no" names the text's own no; "not covered"
-- or "this Act does not say" is where the text is silent or leaves the point to a relevant Act or the rules (NOTES.md).

§ `South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 -- the applicant`

DECLARE `Tribunal party` IS ONE OF `I, the applicant`, `the decision-maker`
DECLARE `Tribunal step` IS ONE OF
    `give the Tribunal the statement of reasons and the relevant documents`
    `apply for an internal review`
    `institute the appeal`

§§ `Which jurisdiction my matter is in (ss 32-34, regulations r 5)`

-- r 5(1) takes these decisions out of "reviewable decision"; r 5(2) prescribes these as reviewable decisions.
DECLARE `Kind of decision` IS ONE OF
    `part of a residential park dispute under the Residential Parks Act 2007`
    `part of a tenancy dispute under the Residential Tenancies Act 1995`
    `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 1987 s 31(8) or in a dispute with a resident`
    `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 2016 s 44(8) or in a dispute with a resident`
    `a community housing decision under the Community Housing Providers (National Law) Schedule 2 clause 2 or a transitioning co-operative's s 84 application`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Who made the decision` IS ONE OF
    `the Crown, a government agency or instrumentality of the Crown`
    `a person or body prescribed by the regulations`
    `someone else`

-- s 34(2): a reviewable decision is "(a) a decision made by the Crown or an agency or instrumentality of the Crown; or
-- (b) a decision made by a prescribed person or body; or (c) a prescribed decision or class of decision, but does not
-- include a decision ... excluded by the regulations". r 5(1) excludes the first four kinds "even if" the Crown made
-- them; r 5(2) prescribes the fifth. s 34(2)(b) "a prescribed person or body" is kept as a choice for a later
-- regulation, but the deposited Regulations prescribe no person or body (r 5(2) prescribes classes of decision), so on
-- the deposited text that choice is never the right one (finding 10).
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who made the decision`, `kind` IS A `Kind of decision`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 34(2) -- a reviewable decision` `who` `kind` IF
        (    `who` EQUALS `the Crown, a government agency or instrumentality of the Crown`
         OR  `who` EQUALS `a person or body prescribed by the regulations`
         OR  `kind` EQUALS `a community housing decision under the Community Housing Providers (National Law) Schedule 2 clause 2 or a transitioning co-operative's s 84 application`)
    AND (NOT `excluded by r 5(1)`)
    WHERE
        `excluded by r 5(1)` MEANS
               `kind` EQUALS `part of a residential park dispute under the Residential Parks Act 2007`
            OR `kind` EQUALS `part of a tenancy dispute under the Residential Tenancies Act 1995`
            OR `kind` EQUALS `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 1987 s 31(8) or in a dispute with a resident`
            OR `kind` EQUALS `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 2016 s 44(8) or in a dispute with a resident`

DECLARE `Jurisdiction answer` IS ONE OF
    `review jurisdiction: the Tribunal rehears the decision and must reach the correct or preferable decision, giving appropriate weight to the original decision (s 34(1), (3), (4))`
    `original jurisdiction: the Tribunal decides the matter itself, resolves the dispute or takes another course it considers appropriate (s 33)`

-- s 34(1): review jurisdiction where the matter "expressly or necessarily involves a review of a decision (a reviewable
-- decision)"; s 33(1): original jurisdiction where the matter "does not involve a reviewable decision within the meaning
-- of section 34". So a decision that is reviewed but is not a "reviewable decision" (a public landlord's tenancy
-- decision, r 5(1)(b)) falls in the original jurisdiction.
@export Is my matter in SACAT's review jurisdiction or its original jurisdiction (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 32-34; Regulations 2015 r 5)? Review jurisdiction covers reviews of decisions by the Crown or a government agency, or by prescribed persons or of prescribed kinds; tenancy, residential park and retirement village disputes are excluded even where a government landlord or operator made the decision, and fall in the original jurisdiction.
GIVEN `the Act asks the Tribunal to review a decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act that brings your matter to SACAT gives it a review of a decision someone has made (for example "apply to the Tribunal for a review of the decision"), rather than a claim or dispute for the Tribunal to decide first-hand
      `who made it` IS A `Who made the decision` @desc Who made the decision: the Crown, a government department, agency or instrumentality of the Crown; a person or body prescribed by the regulations; or someone else (a private landlord, a company, a person). The Act lets the regulations prescribe persons or bodies (s 34(2)(b)), but the 2015 Regulations as at 1 July 2020 prescribe none (r 5(2) prescribes kinds of decision, asked separately), so choose this only if a later regulation or a relevant Act names the decision-maker
      `what kind` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc Whether the decision is part of a residential park dispute, a tenancy dispute, a retirement village dispute (1987 or 2016 Act), a community housing provider dispute under the National Law, or none of these
GIVETH A `Jurisdiction answer`
`Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` MEANS
    IF `the Act asks the Tribunal to review a decision` AND `s 34(2) -- a reviewable decision` `who made it` `what kind`
    THEN `review jurisdiction: the Tribunal rehears the decision and must reach the correct or preferable decision, giving appropriate weight to the original decision (s 34(1), (3), (4))`
    ELSE `original jurisdiction: the Tribunal decides the matter itself, resolves the dispute or takes another course it considers appropriate (s 33)`

§§ `The decision-maker's reasons and documents (s 35, regulations r 6)`
-- Situation: you have applied for review of a decision (review jurisdiction).

-- s 35(2): the decision-maker "must provide ... within a reasonable period and in any event within the time prescribed by
-- the regulations: (a) a written statement of the reasons for the decision; (b) any document or thing ... that may be
-- relevant". r 6: "21 days from the time the decision-maker receives notice that an application has been made".
@export I have applied to SACAT for review of a decision: by when must the decision-maker give the Tribunal its reasons and the relevant documents (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 35(2); Regulations 2015 r 6)? Within a reasonable period and in any event within 21 days from receiving notice of your application.
-- r 6's 21 days, written once: the deadline question, the rule's WITHIN and the not-yet-given branch all use it.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`r 6 -- the days the decision-maker has` MEANS 21

GIVEN `notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker received notice that you had applied to SACAT for review
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the decision-maker give its reasons and documents` `notice received on` MEANS `the date` `r 6 -- the days the decision-maker has` `days after` `notice received on`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal step`
`s 35(2) -- the decision-maker must give its reasons and documents within 21 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the decision-maker`
    MUST `give the Tribunal the statement of reasons and the relevant documents`
    WITHIN `r 6 -- the days the decision-maker has`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the decision-maker` BECAUSE "no statement of reasons and documents within 21 days of notice of the application (s 35(2), r 6)"

GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 35(2) -- reasons given on this day are in time` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 35(2) -- the decision-maker must give its reasons and documents within 21 days` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the decision-maker` `give the Tribunal the statement of reasons and the relevant documents` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Where the decision-maker's reasons stand` IS ONE OF
    `given within the 21 days (s 35(2), r 6)`
    `not yet late: the decision-maker has until the 21st day after receiving notice (s 35(2), r 6)`
    `late: the 21 days have passed without them; the Tribunal can require them by written notice (s 35(2), (4)-(6))`

@export Has the decision-maker given SACAT its reasons and documents in time (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 35; Regulations 2015 r 6)? The outer limit is 21 days from receiving notice of your application; the Act also asks for a reasonable period, which only the Tribunal can judge.
GIVEN `notice received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision-maker received notice of your application for review
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision-maker has given the Tribunal the written statement of reasons and the relevant documents
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day they were given (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when they have not been given)
GIVETH A `Where the decision-maker's reasons stand`
`Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` MEANS
    IF `given`
    THEN IF `s 35(2) -- reasons given on this day are in time` (`the days from` `notice received on` `to` `given on`)
         THEN `given within the 21 days (s 35(2), r 6)`
         ELSE `late: the 21 days have passed without them; the Tribunal can require them by written notice (s 35(2), (4)-(6))`
    ELSE IF `the days from` `notice received on` `to` `today` AT MOST `r 6 -- the days the decision-maker has`
         THEN `not yet late: the decision-maker has until the 21st day after receiving notice (s 35(2), r 6)`
         ELSE `late: the 21 days have passed without them; the Tribunal can require them by written notice (s 35(2), (4)-(6))`

§§ `Does a review or appeal stop the decision (ss 36, 73)`

DECLARE `Which proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `my application to SACAT for review of a decision-maker's decision (s 36)`
    `an internal review or appeal against a decision of SACAT (s 73)`

DECLARE `Stay answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the proceedings run (s 36(1))`
    `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the internal review or appeal runs (s 73(1))`
    `yes: the relevant Act says the decision does not operate while it is reviewed (s 36(1)(a))`
    `stayed or varied: an order under s 36(2) stays or varies the decision, on its conditions`
    `stayed: an order under s 73(2) stays the decision until the proceedings are finally decided, on its conditions`

-- s 36(1): commencing review "does not affect the operation of the decision ... unless (a) the relevant Act states
-- otherwise; or (b) an order is made under subsection (2)". s 73(1)-(2): the same for Part 5, with an order of the
-- Tribunal (a legally qualified member) or the Supreme Court; no relevant-Act exception.
@export Does applying for review, internal review or an appeal stop the decision from operating (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 36, 73)? No, unless an order stays it or (for a review of a decision-maker's decision) the Act that gives the review says otherwise.
GIVEN `which` IS A `Which proceedings` @desc Whether you have applied to SACAT to review a government or other decision-maker's decision, or have sought an internal review or an appeal against a decision of SACAT itself
      `the relevant Act says it is suspended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a review of a decision-maker's decision only: whether the Act that gives SACAT the review says the decision does not operate while it is reviewed
      `a stay order has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal, the decision-maker (for s 36) or the Supreme Court (for s 73) has made an order staying or varying the decision pending the outcome
GIVETH A `Stay answer`
`Does the review or appeal stop the decision` MEANS
    IF `which` EQUALS `my application to SACAT for review of a decision-maker's decision (s 36)`
    THEN BRANCH IF `the relevant Act says it is suspended` THEN `yes: the relevant Act says the decision does not operate while it is reviewed (s 36(1)(a))`
                IF `a stay order has been made` THEN `stayed or varied: an order under s 36(2) stays or varies the decision, on its conditions`
                OTHERWISE `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the proceedings run (s 36(1))`
    ELSE IF `a stay order has been made`
         THEN `stayed: an order under s 73(2) stays the decision until the proceedings are finally decided, on its conditions`
         ELSE `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the internal review or appeal runs (s 73(1))`

§§ `What the review decides, and whether it can be reviewed again (ss 37, 38)`
-- Situation: the Tribunal has decided your review, or invited the decision-maker to reconsider.

DECLARE `What happened on my review` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal affirmed the decision`
    `the Tribunal varied the decision`
    `the Tribunal set the decision aside and substituted its own`
    `the Tribunal sent the matter back and the decision-maker has made a decision on reconsideration`
    `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker affirmed its decision`
    `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker varied its decision or substituted a new one`

DECLARE `Further review answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: the decision as affirmed, varied or substituted by the Tribunal is not again open to review before the Tribunal; you may appeal under s 71 (s 37(5))`
    `yes: a decision made on reconsideration after the Tribunal sent the matter back is open to review by the Tribunal (s 37(2))`
    `your review goes on: unless you withdraw it, it is now a review of the varied or substituted decision (s 38(3))`
    `your review goes on: the decision-maker affirmed, so the Tribunal resumes the review of the same decision (s 38(2)(a), (4))`

-- s 37(5), s 37(2), s 38(2)-(4).
@export After SACAT's review, or the decision-maker's reconsideration, can I have the decision reviewed again (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 37, 38)? A decision the Tribunal affirms, varies or substitutes cannot be reviewed again (only appealed); a fresh decision made after the Tribunal sends the matter back can be.
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened on my review` @desc What became of the decision: the Tribunal affirmed it, varied it or substituted its own; the Tribunal sent it back and the decision-maker reconsidered; or, on the Tribunal's invitation during the review, the decision-maker affirmed, varied or replaced it
GIVETH A `Further review answer`
`Can the decision be reviewed again` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what happened`
    WHEN `the Tribunal sent the matter back and the decision-maker has made a decision on reconsideration` THEN `yes: a decision made on reconsideration after the Tribunal sent the matter back is open to review by the Tribunal (s 37(2))`
    WHEN `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker affirmed its decision` THEN `your review goes on: the decision-maker affirmed, so the Tribunal resumes the review of the same decision (s 38(2)(a), (4))`
    WHEN `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker varied its decision or substituted a new one` THEN `your review goes on: unless you withdraw it, it is now a review of the varied or substituted decision (s 38(3))`
    OTHERWISE `no: the decision as affirmed, varied or substituted by the Tribunal is not again open to review before the Tribunal; you may appeal under s 71 (s 37(5))`

DECLARE `Effect date answer` IS ONE OF
    `from when the reviewed decision took, or would have taken, effect (s 37(3)(b))`
    `from the time the relevant Act says (s 37(3)(b))`
    `from the time the Tribunal orders (s 37(3)(b))`

-- s 37(3): the decision as affirmed, varied or substituted "is to be regarded as ... a decision of the decision-maker;
-- and (b) unless the relevant Act states otherwise or the Tribunal orders otherwise, is to be regarded as having effect,
-- from the time when the decision reviewed would have, or would have had, effect".
@export From when does SACAT's decision on my review take effect (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 37(3))? It is treated as the decision-maker's decision and, unless the relevant Act or the Tribunal says otherwise, takes effect from when the original decision did, or would have.
GIVEN `the relevant Act sets the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act that gives the review says when the Tribunal's decision takes effect
      `the Tribunal ordered a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal ordered a different time for its decision to take effect
GIVETH A `Effect date answer`
`From when does the review decision take effect` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the relevant Act sets the time` THEN `from the time the relevant Act says (s 37(3)(b))`
           IF `the Tribunal ordered a time` THEN `from the time the Tribunal orders (s 37(3)(b))`
           OTHERWISE `from when the reviewed decision took, or would have taken, effect (s 37(3)(b))`

§§ `If my application involves federal jurisdiction (Part 3A)`
-- Situation: the Tribunal has transferred your application to the Magistrates Court because deciding it may involve
-- federal jurisdiction (for example, a party lives in another State).

DECLARE `Transfer limitation answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal, which was within the limitation period (s 38C(1), (2))`
    `no: you lodged with the Tribunal after the limitation period expired, so s 38C(2) does not override it`
    `no limitation period applies: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal (s 38C(1))`

-- s 38C(1): "taken to have been commenced in the Magistrates Court on the day on which the application ... was first made
-- to the Tribunal". (2): "applies despite any limitation period ... provided it was lodged with the Tribunal before the
-- expiry of the period". Lodging on the last day of the period is before it expires (fork F2).
@export SACAT transferred my application to the Magistrates Court because of federal jurisdiction: does my claim still count as made in time (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 38C(1), (2))? It is taken to have started on the day you first applied to the Tribunal, despite any limitation period, provided you lodged before the period expired.
GIVEN `lodged on` IS A DATE @desc The day your application was first made (lodged) with SACAT
      `a limitation period applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a limitation period (under the Limitation of Actions Act 1936 or the Act your claim is under) applies to your claim
      `the last day of the limitation period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of that limitation period (if none applies, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Transfer limitation answer`
`Does my transferred claim count as made in time` MEANS
    IF (NOT `a limitation period applies`)
    THEN `no limitation period applies: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal (s 38C(1))`
    ELSE IF `the day` `lodged on` `is on or before` `the last day of the limitation period`
         THEN `yes: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal, which was within the limitation period (s 38C(1), (2))`
         ELSE `no: you lodged with the Tribunal after the limitation period expired, so s 38C(2) does not override it`

DECLARE `Transfer fee answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: the fee is the Tribunal's application fee, and no fee is payable for the transfer itself (s 38B(5), (6))`
    `yes: the Magistrates Court has determined that additional fees are payable because the claims have substantially changed (s 38B(6))`

@export Must I pay another fee because SACAT transferred my application to the Magistrates Court (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 38B(5), (6))? No, unless the Court determines that additional fees are payable because the nature of the claims has substantially changed.
GIVEN `the Court determined additional fees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Magistrates Court has determined that additional fees are payable under the Magistrates Court Act 1991 because of a substantial alteration in the nature of the claims
GIVETH A `Transfer fee answer`
`Must I pay another fee for the transfer` MEANS
    IF `the Court determined additional fees`
    THEN `yes: the Magistrates Court has determined that additional fees are payable because the claims have substantially changed (s 38B(6))`
    ELSE `no: the fee is the Tribunal's application fee, and no fee is payable for the transfer itself (s 38B(5), (6))`

§§ `Who may appear for me, and at the hearing (ss 29, 56, 60)`

DECLARE `Who appears` IS ONE OF
    `myself`
    `counsel (a legal practitioner)`
    `another representative (not a lawyer)`

DECLARE `Representation answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a party may appear personally (s 56(1)(a))`
    `yes: a party may appear by counsel (s 56(1)(b))`
    `yes: the Tribunal has given leave, subject to the rules (s 56(1)(c))`
    `no: a representative other than counsel needs the Tribunal's leave (s 56(1)(c))`
    `no: that person may not act as a representative (s 56(4))`
    `the relevant Act decides: s 56(1) applies subject to it`

-- s 56(1) "entitled to appear (subject to the provisions of a relevant Act) (a) personally; or (b) by counsel; or (c)
-- with the leave of the Tribunal and subject to the rules--by other representative". (4): a person may not act as a
-- representative if a suspended practising certificate, struck off, or acting inconsistently with Legal Practitioners
-- Act 1981 disciplinary proceedings.
@export Who can appear for me at SACAT (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 56)? You may appear yourself or by a lawyer; anyone else needs the Tribunal's leave; a lawyer whose practising certificate is suspended, who has been struck off, or whose acting would be inconsistent with disciplinary proceedings may not represent you. The Act under which your matter comes may limit this.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who appears` @desc Who would appear for you: yourself, counsel (a legal practitioner), or another representative (a relative, an advocate, an agent)
      `the relevant Act limits representation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act that brings your matter to SACAT has its own rule about who may represent a party (for example, limits on lawyers in some tenancy matters)
      `the Tribunal gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another representative: whether the Tribunal has given leave for them to appear
      `the representative is barred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative's practising certificate is suspended, their name has been struck off the roll, or acting would be inconsistent with disciplinary proceedings under the Legal Practitioners Act 1981
GIVETH A `Representation answer`
`Who can appear for me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the relevant Act limits representation` THEN `the relevant Act decides: s 56(1) applies subject to it`
           IF `who` EQUALS `myself` THEN `yes: a party may appear personally (s 56(1)(a))`
           IF `the representative is barred` THEN `no: that person may not act as a representative (s 56(4))`
           IF `who` EQUALS `counsel (a legal practitioner)` THEN `yes: a party may appear by counsel (s 56(1)(b))`
           IF `the Tribunal gave leave` THEN `yes: the Tribunal has given leave, subject to the rules (s 56(1)(c))`
           OTHERWISE `no: a representative other than counsel needs the Tribunal's leave (s 56(1)(c))`

DECLARE `Friend answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may be assisted by another person as a friend (s 56(2))`
    `no: the Tribunal has determined otherwise (s 56(2))`

@export Can someone sit with me and help me as a friend at SACAT (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 56(2))? Yes, unless the Tribunal determines otherwise.
GIVEN `the Tribunal determined otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has determined that you may not be assisted by a friend
GIVETH A `Friend answer`
`Can a friend help me at the hearing` MEANS
    IF `the Tribunal determined otherwise`
    THEN `no: the Tribunal has determined otherwise (s 56(2))`
    ELSE `yes: you may be assisted by another person as a friend (s 56(2))`

DECLARE `Conflicted member answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 29 does not apply: the member has no conflicting interest`
    `no: the member must not take part, because the President has directed the member to withdraw (s 29(b)(i))`
    `no: the member must not take part, because a party (you or another) does not consent (s 29(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the member must disclose the interest, and may take part because every party consents and the President has not directed withdrawal (s 29)`

-- s 29: a member with "a pecuniary or other interest that could conflict" "(a) must disclose the interest to the parties
-- ... and (b) must not take part ... (i) if the President directs the member to withdraw ...; or (ii) if a party to the
-- proceedings does not consent". Whether the interest could conflict is asked as a fact.
@export A SACAT member hearing my case has an interest that could conflict with their duties: can they still take part (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 29)? They must disclose it to the parties; they must not take part if the President directs them to withdraw or if any party does not consent.
GIVEN `the member has a conflicting interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the member has a financial or other interest that could conflict with the proper performance of their functions in your proceedings
      `the President directed withdrawal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President of the Tribunal has directed the member to withdraw
      `I consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consent to the member hearing (or taking part in hearing) your proceedings
      `every other party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every other party to the proceedings consents
GIVETH A `Conflicted member answer`
`Can a member with a conflict hear my case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the member has a conflicting interest`) THEN `s 29 does not apply: the member has no conflicting interest`
           IF `the President directed withdrawal` THEN `no: the member must not take part, because the President has directed the member to withdraw (s 29(b)(i))`
           IF (NOT (`I consent` AND `every other party consents`)) THEN `no: the member must not take part, because a party (you or another) does not consent (s 29(b)(ii))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the member must disclose the interest, and may take part because every party consents and the President has not directed withdrawal (s 29)`

DECLARE `Public hearing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: proceedings must be heard in public (s 60(1))`
    `no: the Tribunal has directed that the hearing, or that part, be held in private (s 60(2)(e))`
    `the other Act decides: s 60(1) is subject to it`

@export Will my SACAT hearing be held in public (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 60)? Yes, unless the Tribunal directs a private hearing (in the interest of justice, for confidential evidence, to expedite, or for another sufficient reason) or another Act says otherwise.
GIVEN `another Act provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act your matter comes under provides for hearings of this kind in private (for example, guardianship or mental health matters)
      `the Tribunal directed a private hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has directed that the hearing, or part of it, be held in private
GIVETH A `Public hearing answer`
`Will my hearing be in public` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `another Act provides otherwise` THEN `the other Act decides: s 60(1) is subject to it`
           IF `the Tribunal directed a private hearing` THEN `no: the Tribunal has directed that the hearing, or that part, be held in private (s 60(2)(e))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: proceedings must be heard in public (s 60(1))`

§§ `Conferences and mediation (ss 50, 51)`
-- Situation: the Tribunal has sent your matter to a compulsory conference or to mediation.

DECLARE `Settlement step` IS ONE OF `a compulsory conference (s 50)`, `a mediation (s 51)`

DECLARE `Use of what was said answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: evidence of anything said or done at a compulsory conference is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 50(10))`
    `no: evidence of anything said or done at a mediation is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 51(11))`
    `yes: every party consents, so it may be used (ss 50(10), 51(11))`

@export Can what was said or done at my SACAT compulsory conference or mediation be used as evidence at the hearing (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 50(10), 51(11))? No, unless every party consents.
GIVEN `which` IS A `Settlement step` @desc Whether it was a compulsory conference or a mediation
      `every party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all parties to the proceedings (you included) consent to it being used
GIVETH A `Use of what was said answer`
`Can what was said at the conference or mediation be used` MEANS
    IF `every party consents`
    THEN `yes: every party consents, so it may be used (ss 50(10), 51(11))`
    ELSE IF `which` EQUALS `a compulsory conference (s 50)`
         THEN `no: evidence of anything said or done at a compulsory conference is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 50(10))`
         ELSE `no: evidence of anything said or done at a mediation is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 51(11))`

DECLARE `Presiding member answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: the member who presided at the conference is disqualified from hearing the matter (s 50(13))`
    `no: the member who mediated cannot take any further part in the proceedings (s 51(12))`
    `yes: every party agrees to the member continuing (ss 50(13), 51(12))`
    `s 51(12) does not apply: the mediator was not a member of the Tribunal`

@export Can the SACAT member who ran my conference or mediation go on to hear and decide my case (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 50(13), 51(12))? Not unless every party agrees.
GIVEN `which` IS A `Settlement step` @desc Whether it was a compulsory conference or a mediation
      `the mediator was a Tribunal member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a mediation: whether the mediator was a member of the Tribunal (for a conference, answer yes)
      `every party agrees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all parties (you included) agree to that member's continued participation
GIVETH A `Presiding member answer`
`Can the conference member hear my case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `which` EQUALS `a mediation (s 51)` AND (NOT `the mediator was a Tribunal member`) THEN `s 51(12) does not apply: the mediator was not a member of the Tribunal`
           IF `every party agrees` THEN `yes: every party agrees to the member continuing (ss 50(13), 51(12))`
           IF `which` EQUALS `a compulsory conference (s 50)` THEN `no: the member who presided at the conference is disqualified from hearing the matter (s 50(13))`
           OTHERWISE `no: the member who mediated cannot take any further part in the proceedings (s 51(12))`

§§ `Withdrawing, dismissal and starting again (ss 46-49, 62(4))`

DECLARE `Withdrawal answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the rules let you withdraw without leave (s 47(1), (2))`
    `yes: the Tribunal has given leave (s 47(1), (2))`
    `no: you can withdraw only with the Tribunal's leave (s 47(2))`

-- s 47(1) the applicant "may withdraw"; (2) "Unless otherwise provided by the rules, an applicant can only act under
-- subsection (1) with the leave of the Tribunal".
@export Can I withdraw my SACAT application (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 47(1), (2))? Yes, but only with the Tribunal's leave unless the Tribunal's rules provide otherwise. The Tribunal may then dismiss or strike out the proceedings.
GIVEN `the rules let me withdraw without leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the SACAT Rules allow an applicant to withdraw this kind of application without leave
      `the Tribunal gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has given you leave to withdraw
GIVETH A `Withdrawal answer`
`Can I withdraw my application` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rules let me withdraw without leave` THEN `yes: the rules let you withdraw without leave (s 47(1), (2))`
           IF `the Tribunal gave leave` THEN `yes: the Tribunal has given leave (s 47(1), (2))`
           OTHERWISE `no: you can withdraw only with the Tribunal's leave (s 47(2))`

DECLARE `Why it was ended` IS ONE OF
    `I withdrew and it was dismissed (s 47(3))`
    `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))`
    `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, trivial, improper or an abuse of process (s 48)`
    `dismissed because I conducted it so as to disadvantage another party (s 49)`
    `dismissed because I did not give security or an undertaking as ordered (s 62(4))`
    `struck out because another tribunal or court is more appropriate (s 46)`

DECLARE `Starting again answer` IS ONE OF
    `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 48(4))`
    `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 49(4))`
    `this Act sets no bar: it does not stop a new proceeding after this kind of dismissal (the relevant Act, limitation periods and the general law may)`

-- s 48(4), s 49(4): "another proceeding of the same kind in relation to the same matter cannot be commenced before the
-- Tribunal without the leave of a Presidential member". Nothing like it follows ss 46, 47 or 62(4) (fork F3).
@export My SACAT application was dismissed or struck out: can I start the same proceeding again (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 46-49, 62(4))? After a dismissal as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48), or for conduct that disadvantaged another party (s 49), only with the leave of a Presidential member. The Act sets no such bar after the other kinds of dismissal.
GIVEN `why` IS A `Why it was ended` @desc Why the Tribunal dismissed or struck out your proceedings: after you withdrew; for want of prosecution; as frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or an abuse of process; for your conduct disadvantaging another party; for not giving security; or because another forum is more appropriate
GIVETH A `Starting again answer`
`Can I start the proceeding again` MEANS
    CONSIDER `why`
    WHEN `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, trivial, improper or an abuse of process (s 48)` THEN `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 48(4))`
    WHEN `dismissed because I conducted it so as to disadvantage another party (s 49)` THEN `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 49(4))`
    OTHERWISE `this Act sets no bar: it does not stop a new proceeding after this kind of dismissal (the relevant Act, limitation periods and the general law may)`

DECLARE `Who is causing disadvantage` IS ONE OF `me, the applicant`, `another party`

DECLARE `Disadvantage answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 49 does not apply: the Tribunal does not believe a party is conducting the proceedings to another's unnecessary disadvantage`
    `your proceedings may be dismissed or struck out (s 49(2)(a)), and only a Presidential member can let you start again (s 49(4))`
    `the Tribunal may decide the proceedings in your favour and make appropriate orders, or strike the other party out (s 49(2)(b))`

-- s 49(1): applies "if the Tribunal believes that a party ... is conducting the proceedings in a way that unnecessarily
-- disadvantages another party", by conduct such as failing to comply with an order, failing to attend, attempting to
-- deceive. The belief is the Tribunal's: an input.
@export What can SACAT do if a party conducts the proceedings in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 49)? If it is you, the applicant, your proceedings may be dismissed; if it is another party, the Tribunal may decide in your favour or strike that party out.
GIVEN `the Tribunal believes s 49 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal believes a party is conducting the proceedings in a way that unnecessarily disadvantages another (for example by ignoring orders or directions without reasonable cause, failing to attend, attempting to deceive, or conducting them vexatiously)
      `who` IS A `Who is causing disadvantage` @desc Which party the Tribunal believes is causing the disadvantage: you (the applicant) or another party
GIVETH A `Disadvantage answer`
`What can the Tribunal do about conduct causing disadvantage` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the Tribunal believes s 49 applies`) THEN `s 49 does not apply: the Tribunal does not believe a party is conducting the proceedings to another's unnecessary disadvantage`
           IF `who` EQUALS `me, the applicant` THEN `your proceedings may be dismissed or struck out (s 49(2)(a)), and only a Presidential member can let you start again (s 49(4))`
           OTHERWISE `the Tribunal may decide the proceedings in your favour and make appropriate orders, or strike the other party out (s 49(2)(b))`

§§ `Costs (ss 57, 58, 91, regulations rr 7, 11)`

DECLARE `How it ended` IS ONE OF
    `it has not been dismissed or struck out`
    `dismissed after withdrawal (s 47(3))`
    `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))`
    `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))`
    `dismissed, or decided, for conduct causing disadvantage (s 49(2))`
    `dismissed or struck out on another ground`

DECLARE `Party costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `each party bears its own costs (s 57(1))`
    `the relevant Act decides costs (s 57(1), (2))`
    `the Tribunal has ordered costs (s 57(2)); they may include compensation for expenses or loss from the proceedings (s 58(1))`
    `the Tribunal should order costs against the party the dismissal is directed against, unless it finds a good reason not to (s 57(3), r 7)`
    `each party bears its own costs: the Tribunal found a good reason not to order costs (s 57(1), (3))`

-- s 57(1) "Unless otherwise specified in this Act, a relevant Act, or an order ... parties bear their own costs". (3)
-- "if the Tribunal dismisses or strikes out any proceedings in any prescribed circumstances, the Tribunal should also make
-- an order for costs against the party against whom the action is directed unless ... good reason"; r 7 prescribes
-- dismissal under ss 47(4), 48(2), 49(2) (not s 47(3)).
@export Who pays the legal costs of my SACAT proceedings (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 57, 58; Regulations 2015 r 7)? Each party pays its own unless the relevant Act or a Tribunal order says otherwise. Where proceedings are dismissed for want of prosecution, as frivolous or vexatious, or for conduct causing disadvantage, the Tribunal should order costs unless there is a good reason not to.
GIVEN `the relevant Act deals with costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act your matter comes under has its own rule about costs between the parties
      `the Tribunal made a costs order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has ordered a party to pay another party's costs
      `how it ended` IS A `How it ended` @desc Whether and why the proceedings were dismissed or struck out
      `the Tribunal found a good reason not to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is of the opinion that there is a good reason not to order costs in the particular case
GIVETH A `Party costs answer`
`Who pays the legal costs` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the relevant Act deals with costs` THEN `the relevant Act decides costs (s 57(1), (2))`
           IF `the Tribunal made a costs order` THEN `the Tribunal has ordered costs (s 57(2)); they may include compensation for expenses or loss from the proceedings (s 58(1))`
           IF `prescribed by r 7` AND (NOT `the Tribunal found a good reason not to`) THEN `the Tribunal should order costs against the party the dismissal is directed against, unless it finds a good reason not to (s 57(3), r 7)`
           IF `prescribed by r 7` THEN `each party bears its own costs: the Tribunal found a good reason not to order costs (s 57(1), (3))`
           OTHERWISE `each party bears its own costs (s 57(1))`
    WHERE
        `prescribed by r 7` MEANS
               `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed, or decided, for conduct causing disadvantage (s 49(2))`

DECLARE `Jurisdiction of the matter` IS ONE OF `review jurisdiction`, `original jurisdiction`

DECLARE `Tribunal costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: in the original jurisdiction the Tribunal may order a party to pay all or any of the costs of the proceedings (s 91(2))`
    `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the party brought or conducted the proceedings frivolously or vexatiously (s 91(3)(a))`
    `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the proceedings were dismissed or struck out in a prescribed circumstance (s 91(3)(b), r 11)`
    `no: in the review jurisdiction the Tribunal cannot order you to pay the costs of the proceedings (s 91(3))`

-- s 91(1) "costs of proceedings means costs of, or incidental to, any proceedings of the Tribunal, other than the costs of
-- a party"; (3) in the review jurisdiction "the Tribunal cannot make an order under this section against a party unless
-- (a) ... frivolously or vexatiously; or (b) ... prescribed circumstances"; r 11: dismissal under s 47(3), 47(4), 48(2),
-- or an order or determination under s 49(2).
@export Can SACAT order me to pay the Tribunal's own costs of the proceedings (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 91; Regulations 2015 r 11)? In the original jurisdiction, yes. In the review jurisdiction only if you brought or conducted the proceedings frivolously or vexatiously, or they were dismissed after withdrawal, for want of prosecution, as frivolous or vexatious, or for conduct causing disadvantage.
GIVEN `jurisdiction` IS A `Jurisdiction of the matter` @desc Whether your matter is in the Tribunal's review jurisdiction (a review of a government decision) or its original jurisdiction (a claim or dispute)
      `I acted frivolously or vexatiously` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you brought or conducted the proceedings frivolously or vexatiously (as the Tribunal finds)
      `how it ended` IS A `How it ended` @desc Whether and why the proceedings were dismissed or struck out
GIVETH A `Tribunal costs answer`
`Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `jurisdiction` EQUALS `original jurisdiction` THEN `yes: in the original jurisdiction the Tribunal may order a party to pay all or any of the costs of the proceedings (s 91(2))`
           IF `I acted frivolously or vexatiously` THEN `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the party brought or conducted the proceedings frivolously or vexatiously (s 91(3)(a))`
           IF `prescribed by r 11` THEN `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the proceedings were dismissed or struck out in a prescribed circumstance (s 91(3)(b), r 11)`
           OTHERWISE `no: in the review jurisdiction the Tribunal cannot order you to pay the costs of the proceedings (s 91(3))`
    WHERE
        `prescribed by r 11` MEANS
               `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed after withdrawal (s 47(3))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))`
            OR `how it ended` EQUALS `dismissed, or decided, for conduct causing disadvantage (s 49(2))`

DECLARE `Security answer` IS ONE OF
    `you are not in default of a security order`
    `your proceedings may be dismissed with costs, or decided against you with costs (s 62(4))`

@export SACAT ordered me to give security for costs and I have not: what can happen (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 62)? The Tribunal may dismiss the proceedings with costs, or decide them against you with costs.
GIVEN `a security order was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal ordered you to give security for costs, or an undertaking to pay amounts that may be awarded against you
      `I gave it as ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the security or undertaking in the amount, at the time and in the manner and form ordered
GIVETH A `Security answer`
`What happens if I do not give security` MEANS
    IF `a security order was made` AND (NOT `I gave it as ordered`)
    THEN `your proceedings may be dismissed with costs, or decided against you with costs (s 62(4))`
    ELSE `you are not in default of a security order`

§§ `Time limits (s 66)`

DECLARE `Extension answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the rules let the Tribunal extend the time limit, even after it has passed, on conditions it sets (s 66)`
    `not under s 66: this Act lets the Tribunal extend a time limit only where its rules provide for it`

-- s 66(1): "The rules may provide for the Tribunal to extend or abridge a time limit ... even though the limit is imposed
-- under this Act or a relevant Act"; (2) "may be authorised even though the time for complying has passed".
@export I have missed a time limit for starting or taking a step in SACAT proceedings: can the Tribunal give me more time (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 66)? Yes, where the Tribunal's rules provide for it, even after the time has passed and even for a limit set by another Act. Internal reviews and appeals have their own power to allow late applications (ss 70(2), 71(3)).
GIVEN `the rules provide for extending this limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the SACAT Rules allow the Tribunal to extend this kind of time limit
GIVETH A `Extension answer`
`Can the Tribunal give me more time` MEANS
    IF `the rules provide for extending this limit`
    THEN `yes: the rules let the Tribunal extend the time limit, even after it has passed, on conditions it sets (s 66)`
    ELSE `not under s 66: this Act lets the Tribunal extend a time limit only where its rules provide for it`

§§ `Internal review of a SACAT decision (s 70, regulations r 4)`
-- Situation: SACAT has decided your matter and you want it reviewed.

DECLARE `Who decided` IS ONE OF
    `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction`
    `Tribunal members, in the review jurisdiction`
    `a registrar or other staff member, in the original jurisdiction`
    `a registrar or other staff member, in the review jurisdiction`

DECLARE `Internal review answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, with the leave of a legally qualified member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(a), (1a)(a))`
    `yes, with the leave of a Presidential member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(b), (1a)(b))`
    `yes, with leave: the decision is both in the original jurisdiction and by a registrar or staff member, so the Act names both a legally qualified member and a Presidential member (s 70(1a))`
    `no: an order transferring the application to the Magistrates Court cannot be reviewed or appealed under Part 5 (s 38B(7))`
    `no: that interlocutory direction or order is not a "decision" for internal review or appeal (s 3(1), r 4)`
    `not under s 70: a decision of Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction is not open to internal review; it may be appealed (s 71)`

-- s 70(1): review of a decision "(a) in the exercise of its original jurisdiction; or (b) as constituted by a registrar
-- or other member of the staff". (1a): (a) only by leave of a legally qualified member; (b) only by leave of a
-- Presidential member. r 4 excludes adjournments, consolidation directions, s 60(2)(e), (f), (g), (i) directions, s 67
-- determinations and two guardianship steps from "decision" for ss 70 and 71. s 38B(7): transfer orders not reviewable.
@export Can I ask SACAT to review its own decision in my matter (an internal review) (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 70; Regulations 2015 r 4)? Decisions in the original jurisdiction, and decisions made by a registrar or other staff member, can be internally reviewed with leave. Some interlocutory steps (adjournments, directions to consolidate, privacy and suppression directions, electronic or on-the-papers hearings) and transfers to the Magistrates Court cannot.
GIVEN `who decided` IS A `Who decided` @desc Who made the decision and in which jurisdiction: Tribunal members or a registrar or other staff member; original jurisdiction (a claim or dispute) or review jurisdiction (a review of a government decision)
      `it is an excluded interlocutory step` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is an adjournment or refusal to adjourn, a direction to consolidate, split or hear proceedings together, a direction for a private hearing or restricting publication or excluding a person, a decision to hear by phone or video or on the papers, a direction to the Public Advocate to investigate, or authorising publication of a guardianship report
      `it is a transfer to the Magistrates Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the decision is an order transferring your application to the Magistrates Court because of federal jurisdiction
GIVETH A `Internal review answer`
`Can I apply for internal review` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it is a transfer to the Magistrates Court` THEN `no: an order transferring the application to the Magistrates Court cannot be reviewed or appealed under Part 5 (s 38B(7))`
           IF `it is an excluded interlocutory step` THEN `no: that interlocutory direction or order is not a "decision" for internal review or appeal (s 3(1), r 4)`
           IF `who decided` EQUALS `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` THEN `yes, with the leave of a legally qualified member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(a), (1a)(a))`
           IF `who decided` EQUALS `a registrar or other staff member, in the review jurisdiction` THEN `yes, with the leave of a Presidential member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(b), (1a)(b))`
           IF `who decided` EQUALS `a registrar or other staff member, in the original jurisdiction` THEN `yes, with leave: the decision is both in the original jurisdiction and by a registrar or staff member, so the Act names both a legally qualified member and a Presidential member (s 70(1a))`
           OTHERWISE `not under s 70: a decision of Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction is not open to internal review; it may be appealed (s 71)`

-- s 70(2): "must be instituted within 1 month of the making of the decision".
@export By when must I apply for an internal review of a SACAT decision (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 70(2))? Within 1 month of the making of the decision; the Tribunal may allow a later application if satisfied it is just and reasonable.
GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made the decision you want reviewed
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for internal review` `decision made on` MEANS `the last day of one month after` `decision made on`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal step`
`s 70(2) -- I must apply for internal review within 1 month` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the applicant`
    MUST `apply for an internal review`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the applicant` BECAUSE "no internal review application within 1 month of the decision; only the Tribunal can dispense with the time limit (s 70(2))"

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal step`
`s 71(3) -- I must institute the appeal within 1 month` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the applicant`
    MUST `institute the appeal`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I, the applicant` BECAUSE "no appeal instituted within 1 month of the decision; only the Supreme Court can dispense with the time limit (s 71(3))"

GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE, `acted on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 70(2) -- an application on this day is in time` `decision made on` `acted on` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 70(2) -- I must apply for internal review within 1 month` (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` (`the last day of one month after` `decision made on`))) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` `acted on`)), (EVENT `I, the applicant` `apply for an internal review` (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` `acted on`)))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE, `acted on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 71(3) -- an appeal on this day is in time` `decision made on` `acted on` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 71(3) -- I must institute the appeal within 1 month` (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` (`the last day of one month after` `decision made on`))) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` `acted on`)), (EVENT `I, the applicant` `institute the appeal` (`the days from` `decision made on` `to` `acted on`)))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Time answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
    `not yet late: the month has not run out`
    `late, but the time limit has been dispensed with`
    `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`

GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE, `acted` IS A BOOLEAN, `in time` IS A BOOLEAN, `today` IS A DATE, `dispensed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Time answer`
`the one-month time answer` `decision made on` `acted` `in time` `today` `dispensed` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `acted` AND `in time` THEN `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
           IF (NOT `acted`) AND `the day` `today` `is on or before` (`the last day of one month after` `decision made on`) THEN `not yet late: the month has not run out`
           IF `dispensed` THEN `late, but the time limit has been dispensed with`
           OTHERWISE `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`

@export Is my application for internal review of a SACAT decision in time (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 70(2))? It must be made within 1 month of the decision; the Tribunal may dispense with that, even after it has passed, if satisfied it is just and reasonable.
GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made the decision
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for the internal review
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used if you have not applied)
      `the Tribunal dispensed with the time limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal, satisfied it is just and reasonable, has dispensed with the 1-month limit
GIVETH A `Time answer`
`Is my internal review application in time` MEANS
    `the one-month time answer` `decision made on` `applied` (`s 70(2) -- an application on this day is in time` `decision made on` `applied on`) `today` `the Tribunal dispensed with the time limit`

§§ `Appeals to the Supreme Court (ss 71, 73)`

DECLARE `How the Tribunal was constituted` IS ONE OF
    `a Presidential member alone`
    `2 or 3 members including a Presidential member`
    `members none of whom is a Presidential member`
    `a registrar or other staff member`

DECLARE `Appeal court answer` IS ONE OF
    `to the Court of Appeal, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(a), (2))`
    `to a single Judge of the Supreme Court, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(b), (2))`
    `the relevant Act decides: s 71 applies subject to its provisions on review and appeal`

-- s 71(1): appeal "(a) ... constituted of a Presidential member ... or of 2 or 3 members including a Presidential
-- member, to the Court of Appeal; or (b) in any other case--to the Supreme Court constituted of a single Judge". (2):
-- "only by leave of the Supreme Court (but ... may be displaced or modified by ... a relevant Act)". (1a) lets the
-- Supreme Court Rules send a matter to the other of the two; those Rules are not deposited, so the answer is s 71(1)'s
-- default (NOTES.md, finding 6).
@export Which court hears my appeal against a SACAT decision, and do I need leave (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 71(1), (2))? The Court of Appeal if a Presidential member decided (alone or on a panel of 2 or 3); otherwise a single Judge of the Supreme Court. Either way you need the Supreme Court's leave, unless the Act your matter comes under says otherwise.
GIVEN `constituted` IS A `How the Tribunal was constituted` @desc Who made the decision: a Presidential member (the President or a Deputy President) alone; a panel of 2 or 3 including a Presidential member; members none of whom is a Presidential member; or a registrar or staff member
      `the relevant Act provides for appeals` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act your matter comes under has its own provision about review of or appeal against the Tribunal's decision
GIVETH A `Appeal court answer`
`Where does my appeal go` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the relevant Act provides for appeals` THEN `the relevant Act decides: s 71 applies subject to its provisions on review and appeal`
           IF `constituted` EQUALS `a Presidential member alone` OR `constituted` EQUALS `2 or 3 members including a Presidential member` THEN `to the Court of Appeal, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(a), (2))`
           OTHERWISE `to a single Judge of the Supreme Court, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(b), (2))`

DECLARE `Review first answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you cannot appeal until a s 70 internal review of the decision has been conducted (s 71(2a))`
    `no: an internal review has already been conducted (s 71(2a))`
    `no: the requirement is displaced for this decision (s 71(2b))`
    `no: the decision was made by Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction, so s 71(2a) does not apply`

-- s 71(2a): an appeal against a decision "in the exercise of its original jurisdiction, or ... constituted by a
-- registrar or other member of the staff ... may not be instituted ... unless or until a review ... under section 70".
-- (2b): displaced by Supreme Court Rules, regulations or a relevant Act, or by the President's determination.
@export Must I seek an internal review at SACAT before I can appeal to the Supreme Court (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 71(2a), (2b))? Yes for decisions in the original jurisdiction and decisions of a registrar or staff member, unless the requirement is displaced by court rules, regulations, the relevant Act, or the President's determination.
GIVEN `who decided` IS A `Who decided` @desc Who made the decision and in which jurisdiction
      `an internal review has been conducted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a review of the decision under s 70 has already been conducted
      `the requirement is displaced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court Rules, the regulations or the Act your matter comes under displace the requirement for this kind of case, or the President of the Tribunal has determined the decision (or its class) should not need review first
GIVETH A `Review first answer`
`Must I seek internal review before appealing` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `who decided` EQUALS `Tribunal members, in the review jurisdiction` THEN `no: the decision was made by Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction, so s 71(2a) does not apply`
           IF `the requirement is displaced` THEN `no: the requirement is displaced for this decision (s 71(2b))`
           IF `an internal review has been conducted` THEN `no: an internal review has already been conducted (s 71(2a))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: you cannot appeal until a s 70 internal review of the decision has been conducted (s 71(2a))`

@export By when must I appeal to the Supreme Court against a SACAT decision (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 71(3))? Within 1 month of the making of the decision; the Supreme Court may allow a later appeal if satisfied it is just and reasonable.
GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made the decision you are appealing against
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I appeal` `decision made on` MEANS `the last day of one month after` `decision made on`

@export Is my appeal against a SACAT decision in time (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 71(3))? It must be instituted within 1 month of the decision; the Supreme Court may dispense with that, even after it has passed, if satisfied it is just and reasonable.
GIVEN `decision made on` IS A DATE @desc The day the Tribunal made the decision (where it was internally reviewed, the decision on that review)
      `appealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have instituted the appeal
      `appealed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you instituted it (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used if you have not appealed)
      `the Supreme Court dispensed with the time limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court, satisfied it is just and reasonable, has dispensed with the 1-month limit
GIVETH A `Time answer`
`Is my appeal in time` MEANS
    `the one-month time answer` `decision made on` `appealed` (`s 71(3) -- an appeal on this day is in time` `decision made on` `appealed on`) `today` `the Supreme Court dispensed with the time limit`

§§ `Correcting and reopening a decision (ss 84, 85)`

DECLARE `Kind of mistake` IS ONE OF
    `a clerical mistake`
    `an error from an accidental slip or omission`
    `a material miscalculation of figures or a material mistake in describing a person, thing or matter`
    `a defect of form`
    `an error of fact, law or judgment in the decision itself`

DECLARE `Correction answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply, under the rules, for the Tribunal to correct it (s 84)`
    `not under s 84: it reaches only clerical mistakes, slips, material miscalculations or misdescriptions, and defects of form; seek internal review or appeal`

@export SACAT's decision or its reasons contain a mistake: can I ask the Tribunal to correct it (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 84)? Yes for a clerical mistake, an accidental slip or omission, a material miscalculation or misdescription, or a defect of form; not for a disagreement with the decision itself.
GIVEN `the mistake` IS A `Kind of mistake` @desc What kind of mistake it is: clerical; an accidental slip or omission; a material miscalculation of figures or misdescription of a person, thing or matter; a defect of form; or an error in the substance of the decision
GIVETH A `Correction answer`
`Can I have a mistake corrected` MEANS
    IF `the mistake` EQUALS `an error of fact, law or judgment in the decision itself`
    THEN `not under s 84: it reaches only clerical mistakes, slips, material miscalculations or misdescriptions, and defects of form; seek internal review or appeal`
    ELSE `yes: you may apply, under the rules, for the Tribunal to correct it (s 84)`

DECLARE `Absence review answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: s 85 is only for a person who neither appeared nor was represented at a hearing at which, or before which, the decision was made`
    `no: a mediation is not a "relevant hearing" for s 85`
    `no: the application must be made within the time limits in the rules (s 85(3))`
    `you may apply, but the Tribunal is not satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, so s 85(5) does not require it to review the decision`
    `yes: the Tribunal is to review the decision and may revoke or vary it (s 85(5))`

-- s 85(2): "A person in respect of whom the Tribunal makes a decision may apply ... if the person did not appear and was
-- not represented at a relevant hearing"; (1) relevant hearing "does not include mediation"; (3) time limits in the
-- rules; (5) reasonable excuse -> "the Tribunal is to review the decision".
@export SACAT decided my matter at a hearing I did not attend: can I have the decision reviewed (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) s 85)? Yes, if you neither appeared nor were represented, and you apply within the time the rules set. If the Tribunal is satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, it is to review the decision and may revoke or vary it.
GIVEN `the decision is about me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's decision was made in respect of you
      `I appeared or was represented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you appeared (in person or as the Act allows, such as by phone) or were represented at the hearing at which, or before which, the decision was made
      `the only session I missed was a mediation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only session you missed was a mediation (a compulsory conference counts as a hearing; a mediation does not)
      `I applied within the rules' time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied within the time limit set by the SACAT Rules for this kind of application
      `the Tribunal is satisfied I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied you had a reasonable excuse for not attending or being represented
GIVETH A `Absence review answer`
`Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the decision is about me`) OR `I appeared or was represented` THEN `no: s 85 is only for a person who neither appeared nor was represented at a hearing at which, or before which, the decision was made`
           IF `the only session I missed was a mediation` THEN `no: a mediation is not a "relevant hearing" for s 85`
           IF (NOT `I applied within the rules' time`) THEN `no: the application must be made within the time limits in the rules (s 85(3))`
           IF `the Tribunal is satisfied I had a reasonable excuse` THEN `yes: the Tribunal is to review the decision and may revoke or vary it (s 85(5))`
           OTHERWISE `you may apply, but the Tribunal is not satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, so s 85(5) does not require it to review the decision`

§§ `Enforcing an order (ss 38I(1), 89, regulations r 9)`
-- Situation: SACAT has ordered someone to pay you money, or to do something.

DECLARE `Enforcement court answer` IS ONE OF
    `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 89(1), (3)(a), r 9)`
    `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 89(1), (3)(b), r 9)`
    `no: only a party, or a person in whose favour the order was made, is recognised to recover it (s 89(1), r 9)`
    `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(a))`
    `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(b))`
    `no: only the person in whose favour a purported monetary order was made can recover it (s 38I(1))`

-- s 89(1): "the amount ... may be recovered in the appropriate court, by a person recognised by the regulations ... as if
-- it were a debt"; r 9: "A party to the proceedings, or a person in favour of whom the monetary order is otherwise made".
-- (3): Magistrates Court for "an amount that does not exceed" its jurisdictional limit for a monetary claim founded on
-- contract; otherwise the District Court. The limit is set by the Magistrates Court Act 1991, not deposited: an input.
-- s 38I(1): "The amount specified in a purported monetary order made by the Tribunal may be recovered in the appropriate
-- court (within the meaning of section 89) by the person in favour of whom the order was made as if it were a debt".
-- s 38I(5): a purported order is one the Tribunal purportedly made (before or after Part 3A commenced) that is invalid
-- because deciding the application involved federal jurisdiction. Only the person in whose favour it was made, not any
-- party, can recover under (1).
@export SACAT ordered someone to pay me money: where can I recover it (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 38I(1), 89(1), (3); Regulations 2015 r 9)? As a debt, in the Magistrates Court if the amount does not exceed that court's limit for contract claims, otherwise in the District Court. A party, or a person in whose favour the order was made, can do this; for a purported order that is invalid for want of federal jurisdiction, only the person in whose favour it was made.
GIVEN `I was a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a party to the proceedings
      `the order was made in my favour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order was made in your favour
      `the amount ordered` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the order requires to be paid, in dollars (a monetary order includes fines, costs and compensation for breach of an Act)
      `the Magistrates Court contract limit` IS A NUMBER @desc The Magistrates Court's jurisdictional limit for a monetary claim founded on contract, in dollars, under the Magistrates Court Act 1991 (check the current figure)
      `it is a purported order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order is a "purported" order: one the Tribunal made that is invalid because deciding the application involved federal jurisdiction (for example a claim between residents of different States), as s 38I(5) describes (for most orders, no)
GIVETH A `Enforcement court answer`
`Where can I recover the money` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it is a purported order` AND (NOT `the order was made in my favour`) THEN `no: only the person in whose favour a purported monetary order was made can recover it (s 38I(1))`
           IF `it is a purported order` AND `within the Magistrates Court limit` THEN `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(a))`
           IF `it is a purported order` THEN `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(b))`
           IF (NOT (`I was a party` OR `the order was made in my favour`)) THEN `no: only a party, or a person in whose favour the order was made, is recognised to recover it (s 89(1), r 9)`
           IF `within the Magistrates Court limit` THEN `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 89(1), (3)(a), r 9)`
           OTHERWISE `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 89(1), (3)(b), r 9)`
    WHERE
        `within the Magistrates Court limit` MEANS `the amount ordered` AT MOST `the Magistrates Court contract limit`

§§ `Offences a party or witness can commit (ss 38I(2), 40(4), 41(3), 60(3), 89(2), 93A, 93B)`

DECLARE `What I did` IS ONE OF
    `refused or failed to take an oath or affirmation when required (s 40(4)(a))`
    `failed to produce evidentiary material I was required to produce (s 40(4)(b))`
    `failed to appear in answer to a summons (s 40(4)(c))`
    `failed to give evidence or answer a question put in the proceedings (s 40(4)(d))`
    `gave false or misleading evidence to the Tribunal (s 40(4)(e))`
    `obstructed a member or authorised person entering or inspecting property (s 41(3))`
    `did not comply with a direction for a private hearing or restricting publication or disclosure (s 60(3))`
    `did not comply with an order of the Tribunal, other than an order to pay money (s 89(2))`
    `did not comply with a purported order of the Tribunal (one invalid for want of federal jurisdiction), other than a purported monetary order (s 38I(2))`
    `wilfully interrupted proceedings, or used offensive language or behaved in a disorderly or offensive manner (s 93A)`
    `knowingly made a false or misleading statement to have fees waived, remitted or deferred (s 93B)`

DECLARE `Offence answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: that is an offence`
    `no: it is an offence only if done without reasonable excuse, and you had one (s 40(4)(b)-(d))`

-- s 40(4)(b), (c), (d) are "without reasonable excuse"; (a), (e) and the rest are not.
@export Is what I did in SACAT proceedings an offence (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 38I(2), 40(4), 41(3), 60(3), 89(2), 93A, 93B)? Failing to produce material, to answer a summons or to answer questions is an offence only without a reasonable excuse; the others are offences as stated.
GIVEN `what I did` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did or failed to do
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse (as a court would find); it matters only for failing to produce material, to appear on a summons, or to give evidence or answer a question
GIVETH A `Offence answer`
`Is what I did an offence` MEANS
    IF `excuse matters` AND `I had a reasonable excuse`
    THEN `no: it is an offence only if done without reasonable excuse, and you had one (s 40(4)(b)-(d))`
    ELSE `yes: that is an offence`
    WHERE
        `excuse matters` MEANS
               `what I did` EQUALS `failed to produce evidentiary material I was required to produce (s 40(4)(b))`
            OR `what I did` EQUALS `failed to appear in answer to a summons (s 40(4)(c))`
            OR `what I did` EQUALS `failed to give evidence or answer a question put in the proceedings (s 40(4)(d))`

@export What is the largest fine I can be given for this offence in SACAT proceedings, in dollars (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 38I(2), 40(4), 41(3), 60(3), 89(2), 93A, 93B)? $25,000 for summons and evidence offences; $10,000 for obstructing an inspection, breaching a privacy direction or disrupting proceedings; $50,000 for disobeying an order or a purported order; $1,250 for a false statement to get a fee waiver.
GIVEN `what I did` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did or failed to do
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the largest fine` `what I did` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what I did`
    WHEN `obstructed a member or authorised person entering or inspecting property (s 41(3))` THEN 10000
    WHEN `did not comply with a direction for a private hearing or restricting publication or disclosure (s 60(3))` THEN 10000
    WHEN `did not comply with an order of the Tribunal, other than an order to pay money (s 89(2))` THEN 50000
    WHEN `did not comply with a purported order of the Tribunal (one invalid for want of federal jurisdiction), other than a purported monetary order (s 38I(2))` THEN 50000
    WHEN `wilfully interrupted proceedings, or used offensive language or behaved in a disorderly or offensive manner (s 93A)` THEN 10000
    WHEN `knowingly made a false or misleading statement to have fees waived, remitted or deferred (s 93B)` THEN 1250
    OTHERWISE 25000

@export What is the longest prison term I can be given for this offence in SACAT proceedings, in months (0 if the offence carries no imprisonment) (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) ss 38I(2), 40(4), 41(3), 60(3), 89(2), 93A, 93B)? 12 months for summons and evidence offences; 6 months for obstructing an inspection or disrupting proceedings; none for breaching a privacy direction; 24 months for disobeying an order or a purported order; 3 months for a false statement to get a fee waiver.
GIVEN `what I did` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did or failed to do
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest prison term in months` `what I did` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what I did`
    WHEN `obstructed a member or authorised person entering or inspecting property (s 41(3))` THEN 6
    WHEN `did not comply with a direction for a private hearing or restricting publication or disclosure (s 60(3))` THEN 0
    WHEN `did not comply with an order of the Tribunal, other than an order to pay money (s 89(2))` THEN 24
    WHEN `did not comply with a purported order of the Tribunal (one invalid for want of federal jurisdiction), other than a purported monetary order (s 38I(2))` THEN 24
    WHEN `wilfully interrupted proceedings, or used offensive language or behaved in a disorderly or offensive manner (s 93A)` THEN 6
    WHEN `knowingly made a false or misleading statement to have fees waived, remitted or deferred (s 93B)` THEN 3
    OTHERWISE 12

§§ `Fees (regulations r 14)`

DECLARE `Fee kind` IS ONE OF
    `the fee to start proceedings`
    `the fee for referring a residential park, tenancy or rooming house matter`
    `the fee for an internal review under s 70`
    `the fee for a transcript`
    `another prescribed fee`

DECLARE `Fee answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: you are a body exempt from the Tribunal's fees (r 14(1))`
    `no: fees are not payable in proceedings under the Advance Care Directives, Consent to Medical Treatment and Palliative Care, Guardianship and Administration or Mental Health Acts (r 14(2))`
    `no: as the person the proceedings are about, you need not pay the internal review fee (r 14(3))`
    `no: the referral fee is paid by the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor, or in some cases the third party, not by you (r 14(4))`
    `no: the Registrar or a member has waived or remitted it`
    `deferred: payment has been postponed`
    `yes: the fee is payable (the Registrar may waive, remit or postpone it for financial hardship or in the interests of justice, r 14(6))`

-- r 14(1) exempt persons (a Minister, the Commissioner for Consumer Affairs, the Public Advocate, the Commissioner of
-- Police, a public sector employee for an agency, the SA Housing Trust): asked as one fact. (2) no fees (other than the
-- internal review and transcript fees) in the four health and guardianship Acts; (3) no internal review fee there for
-- the person the proceedings are about. (4) who pays a referral fee: for a referral under Residential Parks Act 2007
-- s 29(6a), Residential Tenancies Act 1995 s 63(7) or s 105M(8), the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor
-- if they lodged a written notice of dispute, otherwise "the third party (as referred to in that subsection)"; for a
-- referral under any other provision of those sections, the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor. "Subject
-- to this regulation", so the (1) exemptions and the (6), (7) waivers still apply. (6), (7) waiver, remission, refund,
-- postponement; (8) a registrar or staff member constituting the Tribunal counts as a member for (7).
DECLARE `My part in a referred matter` IS ONE OF
    `the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor`
    `the third party named in the referral subsection`
    `someone else, such as the resident or tenant`
@export Must I pay the SACAT fee (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Regulations 2015 r 14)? Not in guardianship, mental health, advance care directive or medical consent matters (other than for an internal review or a transcript, and the person the proceedings are about pays no internal review fee); a fee for referring a residential park, tenancy or rooming house matter is paid by the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor (or in some cases the third party); otherwise yes, unless the Registrar or a member waives, remits or postpones it.
GIVEN `the fee` IS A `Fee kind` @desc Which fee: the fee to start proceedings; the fee for referring a matter under the Residential Parks Act 2007 s 29 or the Residential Tenancies Act 1995 s 63 or s 105M; the internal review fee; a transcript fee; or another prescribed fee
      `I am an exempt body` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a Minister, the Commissioner for Consumer Affairs, the Public Advocate, the Commissioner of Police, a public sector employee acting for a public sector agency, or the South Australian Housing Trust (for most people, no)
      `it is a guardianship or health matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are under the Advance Care Directives Act 2013, the Consent to Medical Treatment and Palliative Care Act 1995, the Guardianship and Administration Act 1993 or the Mental Health Act 2009
      `I am the person the proceedings are about` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who is the subject of those proceedings
      `waived or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar or a member has waived or remitted the fee
      `postponed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether payment has been postponed
      `my part` IS A `My part in a referred matter` @desc For a referral fee only: whether you are the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor, the third party named in the subsection the matter was referred under, or someone else such as the resident or tenant (ignored for other fees)
      `referred under the third-party subsection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a referral fee only: whether the matter was referred under Residential Parks Act 2007 s 29(6a), Residential Tenancies Act 1995 s 63(7) or s 105M(8) (the subsections about a third party), rather than another provision of those sections
      `the owner lodged a notice of dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a referral under one of those third-party subsections: whether the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor lodged a written notice of dispute
GIVETH A `Fee answer`
`Must I pay the fee` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I am an exempt body` THEN `no: you are a body exempt from the Tribunal's fees (r 14(1))`
           IF `it is a guardianship or health matter` AND (NOT (`the fee` EQUALS `the fee for an internal review under s 70` OR `the fee` EQUALS `the fee for a transcript`)) THEN `no: fees are not payable in proceedings under the Advance Care Directives, Consent to Medical Treatment and Palliative Care, Guardianship and Administration or Mental Health Acts (r 14(2))`
           IF `it is a guardianship or health matter` AND `the fee` EQUALS `the fee for an internal review under s 70` AND `I am the person the proceedings are about` THEN `no: as the person the proceedings are about, you need not pay the internal review fee (r 14(3))`
           IF `the fee` EQUALS `the fee for referring a residential park, tenancy or rooming house matter` AND (NOT `r 14(4) puts it on me`) THEN `no: the referral fee is paid by the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor, or in some cases the third party, not by you (r 14(4))`
           IF `waived or remitted` THEN `no: the Registrar or a member has waived or remitted it`
           IF `postponed` THEN `deferred: payment has been postponed`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the fee is payable (the Registrar may waive, remit or postpone it for financial hardship or in the interests of justice, r 14(6))`
    WHERE
        `r 14(4) puts it on me` MEANS
            IF `referred under the third-party subsection` AND (NOT `the owner lodged a notice of dispute`)
            THEN `my part` EQUALS `the third party named in the referral subsection`
            ELSE `my part` EQUALS `the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor`

DECLARE `Fee stay answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your proceedings are stayed until the fee is paid, waived or postponed (r 14(5))`
    `no: the fee has been paid, waived or postponed`
    `no: a Presidential member has determined otherwise (r 14(5))`
    `not under r 14(5): the stay applies only to the application, referral, internal review and listed review fees`

-- r 14(5): "Unless a Presidential member otherwise determines, proceedings ... are stayed until a fee that is payable by a
-- person, being a fee that is prescribed for (a) application for the commencement ...; (b) referral ...; (c) ...
-- internal review ...; (d) application for review ... under [listed Acts] is paid, or payment is waived or postponed".
@export Is my SACAT case on hold until I pay the fee (South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Regulations 2015 r 14(5))? Yes for the application, referral and internal review fees, until the fee is paid, waived or postponed, unless a Presidential member determines otherwise.
GIVEN `the fee` IS A `Fee kind` @desc Which fee is unpaid (a fee for a review under the Local Government Act 1999, the Valuation of Land Act 1971 or the Lobbyists Act 2015 counts as the fee to start proceedings)
      `paid, waived or postponed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fee has been paid, or its payment waived or postponed
      `a Presidential member determined otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Presidential member has determined that the proceedings are not stayed
GIVETH A `Fee stay answer`
`Is my case on hold until I pay` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the fee` EQUALS `the fee for a transcript` OR `the fee` EQUALS `another prescribed fee` THEN `not under r 14(5): the stay applies only to the application, referral, internal review and listed review fees`
           IF `paid, waived or postponed` THEN `no: the fee has been paid, waived or postponed`
           IF `a Presidential member determined otherwise` THEN `no: a Presidential member has determined otherwise (r 14(5))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: your proceedings are stayed until the fee is paid, waived or postponed (r 14(5))`
